From 15596504471afee68397e75d05d6cfd5630b5fa0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:17:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] SDR output gamut when HDR is mapped to SDR:
 BT.709, BT.2020 or DCI-P3

A projector or a TV that takes SDR in a wider gamut got HDR mapped to SDR in
BT.709 only, as Kodi sends every SDR picture (asked for by JonnyVee in the
LibreELEC forum, thread 30682, posts 175 and 195).

New setting videoscreen.hadessdrhdrgamut (System > Display, expert level):
BT.709 (the default: nothing changes), BT.2020 or DCI-P3 (D65). It applies to
every HDR picture mapped to SDR, by the bridge (Dolby Vision rebuilt for SDR,
HDR10, HDR10+ and HLG mapped with the tone mapping method of a reference
display) and by Kodi's own shaders (PQ tone mapped with Kodi's methods, HLG):

- the window system says, before the bridge's mapping is taken, which
  container can be used (SdrMappedContainer()): only where the EDID declares
  it (BT2020_RGB, or DCI-P3 RGB: SMPTE ST 2113 RGB), the connector offers its
  Colorspace value (BT2020_RGB, DCI-P3_RGB_D65), the output is not in 3D and
  native Dolby Vision does not hold it; elsewhere BT.709, and kodi.log says
  why;
- the bridge maps into that container (the mapping's gamut), Kodi's shaders
  draw into it (DrawnPrimaries());
- the output is set up as for an SDR video of BT.2020: the display told the
  container and SDR (the HDR InfoFrame of SDR), and the GUI and the subtitles
  composited over the picture converted into it (SetSdrWide() with the
  container); where that cannot be set up, the bridge maps into BT.709 again;
- it ends as SDR in BT.2020 ends: when the picture is no longer HDR, the
  composite is gone, or the output goes into 3D.

The white and the gamma of the SDR target stay those of
videoscreen.hadesdvsdrtarget. kodi.log has one line a video.

The idea of a selector of the SDR gamut is from CroqueMr's build
(https://github.com/CroqueMr/libreelec-CB1/pull/307).
